Recorded before opening docs/design/js.md at e821f9a.

Sources read: spec (particularly 2.4-2.5,4.1,4.5-4.8,9.2,9.4,9.7,10,Appendix B), accepted syntax; relevant runtime D1-D10, request API, declarations, target matrix and spec replacements; multicore capture/portability and keep-env constraints; host concurrency; lazy re-entry environment deltas; meta expansion and sandbox rules; types-joint and std-1 contracts; adopted native revision 3 at lane/note-native, especially N1-N4,N9 and section 10.

Criteria:
1. Standalone language; no keywords and no privileged library identities. A general mechanism must serve three unrelated uses. Types remain sets of ordinary values; providers must not secretly change function types.
2. Principal schemes independent of target/provider selection and traversal order. Service needs discharged explicitly or at a defined root boundary, never opportunistically at library call sites.
3. Unique, explainable provider selection. Define registration scope, priority, duplicates, generic instantiation, dependency closure, cycles, construction/evaluation order, error and cleanup behavior.
4. Ordinary call-site environments and explicit closure capture retained, including keep_env and continuation resume/cancel. A provider's construction environment and its returned functions' call environments must be distinguished.
5. Async classification must account for dependencies, remain stable and conservative enough for actual host starts, preserve synchronous pure exports and completed fakes, and not create async hand-off on blocking WASI.
6. One executable finite deterministic import-reachability algorithm; specify roots, edges, indirect calls, returned functions, fields, aliases, guards and nested functions. No optimizer-dependent acceptance.
7. Target pruning valid under environment substitution/rebinding and higher-order escape. Report precision limits; unavailable bindings checked only after valid pruning, ordinary typechecking still sound.
8. Exact amendments reconcile spec/runtime/native, including declaration legality, top-level initialization, host permissions, imports, entry bootstrap, ABI metadata and macros. Implementation citations must identify real extension seams.

Hard cases:
- Two packages/default and target-specific providers for one service; multiple types in a bundle; generic State(Int)/State(String); self/mutual cycles and target-specific cycles.
- A target-unaware library needing S called from two entries and nested explicit S/Target overrides; factory returns closure with unmet dependency; selected provider itself rebinds dependencies.
- User S provider depends transitively on request service/task runner; sync export uses only fakes, or captured live field; request hidden in callback; WASI same source completes synchronously.
- Target test inline vs alias vs helper, guard, returned closure, recursive reference, aggregate; Target rebound after capture or across a call; target-independent callback called in both native and JS context.
- Unsupported external solely in unused function/provider/branch, in top-level side effects, or in returned function; library metadata alone must not change inference/meaning.
- Implementation must explain every accepted/rejected case without library-name exceptions.
